WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.140
Concurrency safety is about preserving correctness when more than one operation can make progress at the same time.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:19.320
In a C# application, that can mean multiple requests, background jobs, timers, queue consumers, parallel loops, asynchronous continuations, or UI events all touching related state.

3
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.400
The difficult part is that the code can look correct when read from top to bottom.

4
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.860
A method checks a value, changes another value, saves a record, and returns.

5
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:37.880
Under concurrent execution, another operation may observe the same old value, make a competing update, or see state halfway through a transition.

6
00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.780
The problem is not the language syntax.

7
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.760
The problem is the timing between operations.

8
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:58.120
Concurrency bugs can cause lost updates, duplicate work, stale reads, inconsistent state, corrupted data, exhausted resources, deadlocks, thread pool pressure, and unpredictable user behavior.

9
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:08.540
These failures may only appear under load, on different hardware, after a deployment, or when a dependency becomes slower and changes the timing of the system.

10
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:21.700
Security and reliability overlap when concurrency affects authorization decisions, account state, financial operations, quotas, idempotency, workflow approvals, or sensitive data handling.

11
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:29.720
If two operations race through a state transition, the result may violate a business rule even though each operation looked reasonable by itself.

12
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.880
A safe design begins by identifying shared mutable state, ownership, invariants, and resource limits.

13
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.820
What state can change.

14
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.040
Who owns it.

15
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.300
What must always remain true.

16
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.560
Which operations need to be atomic from the application's point of view.

17
00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:51.220
Which resources must be bounded so the system remains available.

18
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.920
Those questions give concurrency review a concrete starting point.
